To adapt the LTE-pretrained Foundation Model to the 5G domain, we introduce a generation-specific configuration tokenizer that converts the 5G configuration schema into token representations compatible with the pretrained configuration encoder. The tokenizer encodes each configuration parameter and projects it into the latent input space established during LTE pre-training, allowing the pretrained configuration backbone to process the new schema without requiring parameter-level correspondence between LTE and 5G configurations.

The KPI branch uses the set of performance indicators consistently available across the LTE and 5G datasets. Its input embedding module, temporal encoder, and KPI reconstruction objective are inherited from LTE pre-training, preserving the temporal representations learned from the source domain. Lightweight representation adapters are introduced where necessary to align the configuration representations with the dimensional interface of the pretrained cross-modal fusion module.

During 5G adaptation, the newly introduced configuration tokenizer and representation adapters are optimized using the original KPI reconstruction objective, while the pretrained configuration encoder, KPI encoder, and cross-attention fusion backbone are retained or only minimally updated. The unavailable attribute modality is represented through an explicit modality mask, enabling the model to operate with the configuration and KPI modalities available in the target domain.

This adaptation strategy enables the model to accommodate a heterogeneous 5G configuration schema and partially available modalities through lightweight interface-level updates, while preserving the network representations and cross-modal relationships learned during LTE pre-training.
